     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-11-10 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>20 years of service recognised: Retired woman IAF officer wins 15-year legal battle for pension as AFT alters ruling</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Personal Finance</span> <span>#Pension</span> <span>#Indian Air Force</span> <span>#Legal Battle</span></div>
       <p>The Armed Forces Tribunal (AFT), Chandigarh Bench, has directed the Union government to treat retired Indian Air Force officer Wing Commander Namrita Chandi as having completed 20 years of pensionable service. This ruling, issued from Chandimandir in Panchkula, makes her eligible for pension and other retirement benefits, modifying the AFT's earlier 2015 judgment. According to The Tribune, her pension should be granted with effect from the day following the completion of 20 years of service.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/20-years-of-service-recognised-retired-woman-iaf-officer-win.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Wing Commander Chandi initially received a Short Service Commission (SSC) in the Indian Air Force in 1996 for an initial tenure of 10 years. Before completing this tenure, she applied for Permanent Commission (PC). However, her request was denied, and she was offered a five-year extension, as the applicable rules did not provide for Permanent Commission to SSC officers in the flying branch. She subsequently retired from service in 2011.</p><p>Chandi challenged the denial of Permanent Commission by filing a writ petition before the Delhi High Court in 2011, a matter transferred to the Principal Bench of the AFT in 2014. In 2015, the tribunal allowed her petition, relying on the Delhi High Court's judgment in Babita Puniya and Others Vs. The Secretary and Another. The Union government challenged this decision before the Supreme Court, but its appeal was dismissed in 2019.</p><p>Following a legal notice from Chandi, Air Force authorities decided to examine her suitability for Permanent Commission. An independent board of officers was constituted, and she was asked to undergo a medical examination on July 22, 2020. However, Chandi objected to being assessed under the selection criteria prescribed in the 2004 and 2010 policies, particularly as she had been out of service for nearly nine years.</p><p>Representing Chandi, advocate Sandeep Bansal sought modification of the tribunal's 2015 order, relying on Supreme Court decisions concerning Permanent Commission for women officers, including Babita Puniya and Lt Cdr Annie Nagaraja and Others. The AFT bench, comprising Justice Sudhir Mittal and Lieutenant General (Dr) Ranbir Singh (retd), rejected the Union government's adjournment request, noting the Supreme Court’s relevant orders were well known. The tribunal clarified that women officers denied consideration for Permanent Commission and no longer in service should be deemed to have completed the requisite substantive pensionable service, specifically referencing the Lt Cdr Annie Nagaraja judgment.</p><p>For an ordinary saver or taxpayer, this ruling reinforces the principle that long-serving personnel in the armed forces, particularly women who faced systemic barriers to Permanent Commission, are entitled to their full pensionary benefits. It signifies that legal battles can rectify past injustices, ensuring that service years are duly recognized despite initial commission types or policy limitations. This decision provides significant relief to Wing Commander Chandi after a prolonged 15-year legal battle and sets a precedent that could benefit other women officers in similar situations. Watch for similar cases of women officers seeking pensionary benefits.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately captures the legal situation and key figures, though it includes some unnecessary filler in the penultimate paragraph.</p>
