     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Business Standard · 2026-11-10 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Centre doubles open market rice sale target to 5 mn tonnes to cool prices</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Rice</span> <span>#Open Market Sale Scheme</span> <span>#Food Prices</span> <span>#Government Policy</span></div>
       <p>The Centre has doubled its open market rice sale target to 5 million tonnes for the 2026-27 financial year (FY27), up from 2.5 million tonnes, in an effort to augment supplies and cool down domestic prices. According to a statement issued on Tuesday, 2.5 million tonnes of rice had already been sold to private parties through e-auctions under the Open Market Sale Scheme (Domestic) by October 31. This figure is significantly higher than the 1.1 million tonnes sold during the corresponding period of the previous financial year. To further increase supplies and taper down prices, another 2.5 million tonnes is targeted for sale in the remaining five months of this financial year.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/centre-doubles-open-market-rice-sale-target-to-5-mn-tonnes-t.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Sources indicate that the decision to supplement rice supplies from the government’s own inventories comes amid rising retail rice prices, driven by expectations of a production shortage, particularly in southern states. A senior industry official noted that rice production in Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Tamil Nadu, and Kerala is estimated to be around 30 percent down due to drought and low rains. Overall India production is expected to be approximately 10 percent less than last year. The official highlighted that the shortage is particularly severe in premium rice varieties such as ponni and sona masuri, which are primarily grown in northern Karnataka, and farmers are reportedly shifting away from growing high-yielding varieties in favor of fine rice.</p><p>As of October 1, 2026, India’s Central pool held around 38 million tonnes of rice, which is almost 280 percent more than the buffer stocking norm of 10.25 million tonnes. This substantial reserve allows the central government to intervene and help stabilize retail rice prices. In related measures, on November 6, the government made it mandatory for all traders, wholesalers, retailers, large chain retailers, and processors or millers across the country to disclose their rice and paddy stocks every Friday on the Food Stock Portal. The directive requires entities to declare stocks under six specific categories: basmati rice, non-basmati premium rice, non-basmati rice, parboiled rice, broken rice, and paddy. Entities failing to register or violating prescribed stock limits face punitive action under sections 6 and 7 of the Essential Commodities Act, 1955, as stated in the announcement.</p><p>To further improve availability, especially in southern states, the Centre has permitted Andhra Pradesh, Karnataka, Kerala, Tamil Nadu, and Telangana to impose stock limits on rice and paddy wherever necessary. These stock limits will remain in force until January 31, 2027, with both the Centre and state governments jointly monitoring their implementation to prevent hoarding and black marketing. Additionally, the government has decided to allocate extra quantities of Bharat Rice to three agencies—the National Agricultural Cooperative Marketing Federation of India (NAFED), the National Cooperative Consumers’ Federation of India (NCCF), and Kendriya Bhandar. These agencies have been directed to explore additional distribution channels to expand the availability of Bharat Rice, particularly in southern states, and improve consumer access to the staple at affordable prices.</p><p>What to watch: The impact of these measures on retail rice prices and market stability.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The report thoroughly covers the government's rice sale targets, supply concerns, and the regulatory actions taken to manage stock and stabilize prices.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.business-standard.com/industry/agriculture/centre-doubles-open-market-rice-sale-target-to-5-mn-tonnes-to-cool-prices-126111000857_1.html" rel="noopener">Business Standard</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
