       <h1>Congressional report blames federal failures for Epstein crimes</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#world</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A 15-month investigation by a Republican-led House committee concludes that federal authorities could have prevented crimes committed by Jeffrey Epstein after 2005. The report cites systemic mishandling by the US Attorney’s Office and Epstein's influence as primary factors.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/congressional-report-blames-federal-failures-for-epstein-cri.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The committee also identified institutional failures, including staffing shortages and poor security, that led to Epstein's 2019 death in a New York prison. Additionally, the report alleges financial institutions enabled human trafficking, and it calls for new legislation to curb the use of non-disclosure agreements to silence victims.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief provides a clear, balanced summary of the congressional report and its primary accusations within the target length.</p>
