       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · News18 World · 2026-11-10 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Eisenkot calls for emergency government as he slams Netanyahu</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Israel</span> <span>#Politics</span> <span>#Netanyahu</span> <span>#Eisenkot</span></div>
       <p>Gadi Eisenkot, leader of the Yashar party, has formally declared caretaker Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu unfit for office, calling on lawmakers to establish an emergency government. Eisenkot’s demand follows a speech by Netanyahu at the opening session of the new parliament, in which the Prime Minister warned of international pressure regarding Iran’s nuclear programme and urged the formation of a national unity government.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/eisenkot-calls-for-emergency-government-as-he-slams-netanyah.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Eisenkot rejected the appeal, describing Netanyahu’s address as &quot;delusional, divisive, hate-filled and shameful.&quot; He accused the Prime Minister of exploiting a state occasion to exacerbate national divisions. Eisenkot, who is actively seeking to build an alternative coalition, argued that Netanyahu’s continued leadership represents a direct threat to Israel’s security.</p><p>The political standoff coincides with significant parliamentary moves by Eisenkot’s bloc, which has gained influence in the new legislature sworn in on Tuesday. Eisenkot’s party has initiated plans to establish a commission of inquiry into the failures that enabled the October 7, 2023, Hamas attack on Israel—a probe Netanyahu has historically resisted. Additionally, the party has announced a proposed repeal of Netanyahu’s controversial judicial overhaul, a policy that previously sparked some of the largest protests in Israeli history.</p><p>As the new parliament convenes, neither Netanyahu, Israel’s longest-serving prime minister, nor Eisenkot commands a clear majority. The current political uncertainty is compounded by the need to form a stable government amidst ongoing security concerns and shifting international dynamics following the recent US elections. Both leaders are now competing to secure the necessary parliamentary support to dictate the legislative agenda and lead the next administration.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately synthesizes the political conflict between Eisenkot and Netanyahu, including the specific parliamentary moves and the context of the new government formation.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.news18.com/world/he-endangers-israels-security-eisenkot-slams-netanyahus-iran-remark-calls-for-emergency-govt-10420758.html" rel="noopener">News18 World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
