     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Business Standard · 2026-11-10 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>European carmakers may set up manufacturing units in India</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#India</span> <span>#EU</span> <span>#Trade</span> <span>#Automotive</span></div>
       <p>European carmakers may establish manufacturing units in India following the implementation of the India-EU free trade agreement (FTA), according to Union Commerce Minister Piyush Goyal. Speaking at a curtain-raiser event for the Bharat Mobility Global Expo in New Delhi, Goyal stated that initial market access granted under the FTA will allow these firms to test demand for specific vehicle models within the domestic market.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/european-carmakers-may-set-up-manufacturing-units-in-india.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Once demand is assessed, the minister indicated that these manufacturers would likely establish local production facilities to serve both India and global markets. Goyal noted that European automakers are increasingly recognizing that their domestic regulatory burdens and high manufacturing costs hinder their competitiveness, prompting a strategic shift toward India. The India-EU FTA, which includes phased tariff concessions on cars under a limited annual quota, is scheduled to be signed by December and is expected to take effect in the first half of 2027.</p><p>This development is part of a broader push by India to expand its trade footprint. India has concluded eight trade agreements over the last six years, and the government estimates its current FTAs could provide preferential access to nearly 75 per cent of the global gross domestic product. Simultaneously, India is negotiating an interim bilateral trade agreement with the United States. While negotiations began in March 2023 and an initial framework was reached in February, the US Supreme Court later struck down the legal basis for reciprocal tariff cuts previously agreed upon, leading to a new round of talks to finalize updated terms. The US remains India’s largest export destination, accounting for approximately 20 per cent of annual shipments, with a trade deficit of around $34 billion recorded in 2025-26.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft captures all key details regarding the India-EU FTA, the minister's comments, and the broader trade context. It accurately reflects the source's information.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.business-standard.com/economy/news/european-carmakers-may-set-up-manufacturing-units-in-india-soon-goyal-126111000995_1.html" rel="noopener">Business Standard</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
