     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · BS Personal Finance · 2026-11-10 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Foreign Investors Return to Indian Markets, Ending 10-Month Outflow Streak</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#India</span> <span>#Finance</span> <span>#FPI</span> <span>#Economy</span></div>
       <p>Foreign Portfolio Investors (FPIs) have reversed a prolonged trend of capital outflows from Indian financial markets, injecting significant funds into equities and government bonds in early November. In the first nine days of November, FPIs purchased ₹53,172 crore of equities and ₹7,480 crore of government bonds. This marks an end to a 10-month streak of monthly equity outflows, signalling renewed overseas interest in domestic assets, according to BS Personal Finance.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/foreign-investors-return-to-indian-markets-ending-10-month-o.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The shift comes after a period of substantial withdrawals. Between January and October, FPIs pulled ₹4.48 trillion from Indian equities, contributing to a 2 per cent decline in the Nifty 50 over the previous year and weighing on the rupee. Recent outflows included ₹45,162 crore in October and ₹44,013 crore in September. The bond market also experienced selling, with outflows of ₹9,192 crore in September and ₹5,609 crore in October, though the November inflow is the highest since July's ₹7,581 crore. Net purchases in FY27 debt, including November, have reached about ₹52,939 crore, largely driven by ₹41,774 crore in June.</p><p>Several factors appear to have driven this reversal. The source indicates that a reduction in the perceived risk of a further escalation in the West Asia conflict, following the US midterm elections, has eased crude oil prices and tempered concerns about an inflationary shock. This improving global environment, coupled with India’s healthy macroeconomic fundamentals, strong corporate earnings, and a meaningful correction in valuations, has made Indian assets more attractive. FPIs have also become less bearish on equity derivatives, with their long-short ratio reaching 26.8 per cent, its second-highest level this year after February 23, as noted by Deven Mistry and Aanshul Agarawal of Motilal Oswal Financial Services.</p><p>The renewed interest has coincided with a sharp rebound in domestic markets. The Nifty 50 rose 5 per cent over the past five trading sessions, and the rupee gained 1.09 per cent against the dollar last week, moving past the Rs 94-a-dollar mark to its strongest level in over four months. For an ordinary saver or taxpayer, a strengthening rupee can make imports cheaper, potentially easing inflationary pressures. Falling bond yields, while impacting fixed-income returns, can signal increased confidence in the economy. Banking system liquidity was in surplus by ₹2.3 trillion as of November 6. Foreign banks and private sector banks were principal buyers in the bond market, with foreign investors showing comfort in adding longer-dated securities.</p><p>What to watch: The sustainability of FPI inflows and global crude oil price movements.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively synthesizes the source material, capturing the key financial data and market context accurately.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.business-standard.com/markets/news/the-turn-of-the-tide-foreign-investors-return-signalling-longer-india-bet-126111000761_1.html" rel="noopener">BS Personal Finance</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
