     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-10 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Genetic Research Uncovers Two New Howler Monkey Species in Amazon After 85 Years</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Howler Monkeys</span> <span>#Amazon Rainforest</span> <span>#Biodiversity</span> <span>#New Species</span></div>
       <p>Genetic research has uncovered two previously unknown species of howler monkey living in the Amazon rainforest, confirming that the world's largest tropical forest is more biodiverse than scientists previously thought. The study, led by researchers from the University of Salford and international collaborators, identified the new species as Alouatta iroi and Alouatta matogrossensis. This discovery comes 85 years after the last howler species, Alouatta puruensis, was described in 1941.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/genetic-research-uncovers-two-new-howler-monkey-species-in-a.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The research involved analyzing the genomes of 11 howler monkey species across South and Central America. By combining genome-wide analysis with morphological data from museum specimens, scientists constructed the most comprehensive evolutionary history of howler monkeys ever documented. This detailed analysis revealed that what was once considered a single species actually contained distinct evolutionary lineages. The findings, published in Communications Biology, contribute significantly to addressing the Linnean and Wallacean shortfalls, which concern the lack of knowledge about the actual number of existing species and their geographic distributions.</p><p>Tracing the howler monkeys' evolutionary history, researchers found that major rivers such as the Amazon, Rio Branco, and Xingu have acted as natural geographical barriers for millions of years. These barriers prevented populations from intermixing, allowing them to evolve into distinct species on opposite sides of rivers and in isolated forest regions. This phenomenon has been instrumental in generating the extraordinary diversity observed across the Amazon Basin today.</p><p>The study also highlighted that several populations, including these newly recognized species, may be at significant risk of extinction. Many of these newly identified species have never been assessed for conservation status. For instance, Alouatta matogrossensis, found in southern Amazonia, inhabits a region experiencing some of the world's fastest deforestation rates due to cropland expansion and other factors. This rapid habitat loss and fragmentation pose a severe threat to the area's biodiversity.</p><p>Professor Jean Boubli from the University of Salford’s Environmental Research &amp; Innovation Centre emphasized the critical timing of this discovery, noting that increasing deforestation, climate change, and habitat loss threaten many Amazonian species before they are fully understood or even discovered. Dr. Romina Batista, an honorary postdoctoral research associate at the University of Salford, added that the study demonstrates the Amazon still holds remarkable biological secrets, even among well-known animals like howler monkeys, underscoring that understanding existing species is the first step toward their protection.</p><p>Conservation efforts, such as the Reconecta Project led by Fernanda Abra, are working to mitigate these threats. This initiative aims to reduce road mortality in arboreal species and connect forest fragments, directly benefiting species like the newly described Mato Grosso red howler monkey.</p><p>What to watch: Ongoing conservation efforts and further biodiversity assessments in the Amazon Basin.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The report effectively summarizes the genetic findings and conservation implications presented in the source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-howler-monkey-species-amazon-years.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
