     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Guardian Environment · 2026-11-10 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Global Leaders Gather in Turkey for Cop31 Amidst Climate Action Shortfalls</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Climate Change</span> <span>#UN Climate Summit</span> <span>#Cop31</span> <span>#Turkey</span></div>
       <p>Global leaders are convening in Turkey for the UN’s 31st annual climate conference, Cop31, amidst a backdrop of global instability and conflict, according to The Guardian. After a year marked by unprecedented droughts, floods, and wildfires driven by human-caused global heating, 91 heads of government are set to meet to discuss the climate crisis. High-ranking officials and civil society campaigners will engage in a fortnight of speeches, protests, and negotiations, yet many observers are questioning the conference's efficacy, as governments pledged over a decade ago at Cop21 in Paris to limit global heating to 1.5C, but implementation has been &quot;sclerotic.&quot; A UN report from two months prior warned temperatures would reach at least 1.8C even with optimistic projections.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/global-leaders-gather-in-turkey-for-cop31-amidst-climate-act.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The conference, formally known as the 31st Conference of the Parties to the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change, is taking place in the Turkish coastal city of Antalya until November 20. The Antalya Expo centre, temporarily designated UN international territory, will host an estimated 70,000 registered delegates, including Indigenous groups from the Amazon, Australia, and the Pacific, noted for their colorful presence. The Guardian’s environment editor Fiona Harvey, reporting from Turkey, highlighted long-running arguments regarding inclusivity and access to formal negotiating spaces, recalling complaints and a symbolic walkout at Cop26. Harvey also observed that &quot;Four out of five of the last Cops were held in countries with repressive regimes,&quot; limiting external dissent.</p><p>This year's summit sees a rancorous dispute between Turkey and Australia regarding hosting responsibilities. Turkey is the official host, managing voluntary agreements, while Australia leads formal negotiations on just transition and climate finance programs. Harvey described Australia’s role as &quot;the short straw,&quot; lacking the global focus of physical hosting, particularly as climate advocates question Australia’s credibility as a major fossil fuel producer. Harvey noted the irony that both Turkey and Australia share a &quot;mutual admiration for coal.&quot; Formal negotiations at Cop31 will primarily focus on the just transition mechanism to support fossil fuel workers and help poorer countries access climate finance.</p><p>Fiona Harvey sensed &quot;less urgency from global leaders&quot; over Cop31 this year, with notable anticipated absences including the Trump White House, China’s Xi Jinping, and India’s Narendra Modi, alongside French president Emmanuel Macron. Soaring oil and gas prices following the war in Iran have, however, encouraged a shift beyond fossil fuels, leading to an easing of climate-damaging carbon emissions this year, as reported by The Guardian.</p><p>What to watch: Progress on just transition and climate finance mechanisms.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Thorough reporting capturing the key elements of the Guardian briefing and interview.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/10/tuesday-briefing-the-world-is-falling-short-on-climate-action-can-cop31-change-that" rel="noopener">Guardian Environment</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
