     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-11-10 · editor 10/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Hindu Succession Act, 1956: Can a grandson claim a share in property inherited by his father? Supreme Court clarifies</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Hindu Succession Act</span> <span>#Supreme Court</span> <span>#Property Rights</span> <span>#Inheritance</span></div>
       <p>The Supreme Court has clarified that a grandson cannot claim a share by birth in property that his father receives after the grandfather's estate is divided among legal heirs. The court held that once property devolves upon a Hindu male's Class I heirs under Section 8 of the Hindu Succession Act, 1956, and is subsequently partitioned, the share allotted to the father becomes his exclusive property, according to Livemint Money.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/hindu-succession-act-1956-can-a-grandson-claim-a-share-in-pr.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The ruling, delivered on November 6, 2026, in the case of `J. Rajesh (Minor) through his Mother and Natural Guardian J. Gomathi v. M. Kumaran (Dead) through LRs &amp; Others`, clarifies when inherited property can be treated as an individual's exclusive estate rather than joint Hindu family or coparcenary property. A bench of Justices Augustine George Masih and Sunita Agarwal dismissed an appeal by a son who had sought a half share in a 1,811 sq ft plot of land allotted to his father during the partition of his grandfather's estate, N. Ramakrishnan. The father had subsequently sold the property through two sale deeds in 1997.</p><p>The dispute stemmed from a compromise decree dated February 3, 1994, which settled a partition suit filed in 1993 by N. Ramakrishnan's daughter. The grandson, born on January 6, 1994, argued that the land was ancestral property and he had acquired coparcenary rights by birth. He contended that his father's share continued to retain its character as joint family property even after the partition. While the trial court initially favored the grandson, the appellate court reversed the decision, a position upheld by the Madras High Court, leading to the Supreme Court appeal.</p><p>In rejecting the grandson's claim, the Supreme Court distinguished between property inherited under Section 8 of the Hindu Succession Act and coparcenary property. Under Section 8, if a Hindu male dies intestate, his property first devolves upon Class I heirs, including his son, daughter, and widow. A grandson is not an independent Class I heir if his father, the deceased's son, is alive and inherits. The bench referenced `CWT v. Chander Sen`, reiterating that property inherited by a son under Section 8 is held in his individual capacity and does not automatically become Hindu Undivided Family (HUF) property in relation to his own children. The court noted that heirs received their shares as tenants-in-common, and the joint coparcenary ceased after partition, making each heir the exclusive owner. The court emphasized, &quot;The express words of Section 8 of the Hindu Succession Act, 1956 cannot be ignored and must prevail.&quot;</p><p>The ruling means that for families dividing ancestral property, the distinction between property inherited through intestate succession under Section 8 and property continuing as part of a coparcenary is crucial. Merely describing property as ancestral does not automatically establish a child's right to a share. The judgment reinforces that a father who receives property through succession under Section 8 and a subsequent partition can generally deal with his allotted share as its exclusive owner, without requiring his son's consent based on birthright. The grandson had not challenged the 1994 compromise decree or his father's sale deeds, instead seeking partition based on an alleged birthright.</p><p>What to watch: How this ruling influences property inheritance disputes and family partitions.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is comprehensive, well-structured, and correctly explains the legal nuances of the Supreme Court ruling and the specific case details provided.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
