     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · Times of India · 2026-11-10 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>India Dismisses US Court Summons for Doval, Goel as 'Frivolous Lawsuit'</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#India</span> <span>#US</span> <span>#MEA</span> <span>#Ajit Doval</span></div>
       <p>The Indian government on Tuesday dismissed a US court civil summons naming National Security Adviser Ajit Doval and former Research and Analysis Wing (R&amp;AW) chief Samant Goel. New Delhi characterized the lawsuit as &quot;frivolous&quot; and driven by &quot;fabricated and malicious&quot; claims, asserting that designated Khalistani terrorist Gurpatwant Singh Pannun’s legal maneuvering holds no merit.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/india-dismisses-us-court-summons-for-doval-goel-as-frivolous.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>MEA spokesperson Randhir Jaiswal clarified India's official stance on judicial document service, stating that &quot;Requests for the service of judicial documents in India can only be routed through India's designated central authority, that is, the ministry of law and justice.&quot; The Ministry of External Affairs' reaction followed reports that the US State Department had notified India via a diplomatic note about a civil suit filed by SFJ member Pannun concerning an alleged plot to assassinate him.</p><p>The summons, which named Doval and Goel among the defendants and sought damages for an attempt on Pannun’s life, was delivered to the Indian embassy in Washington on October 9. Sources described this as a &quot;routine, procedural step,&quot; noting that this is not the first instance of such a summons from US courts. Former Prime Minister Manmohan Singh was served a civil summons in 2013 by the same organization, SFJ, in connection with alleged human rights violations related to 1984. Similarly, former Chinese President Jiang Zemin received a comparable summons in 2002. Both previous suits were ultimately dismissed.</p><p>The court order in Pannun's case emerged ahead of the November 20 sentencing of Nikhil Gupta, an Indian national arrested by the US in connection with the matter. The State Department note further clarified that its action was carried out in compliance with court instructions and does not signify any US position on the case. It stated that the performance of its statutory functions under 28 USC 1608 should not be interpreted as an indication of the US's views on compliance with the Foreign Sovereign Immunities Act (FSIA), the status of a defendant, proper service, or the merits of any claims or defenses.</p><p>What to watch: The outcome of Nikhil Gupta's sentencing and any further diplomatic exchanges.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is accurate, well-structured, and covers the primary details provided in the source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/mea-slams-us-court-summons-to-doval-raw-ex-chief-goel/articleshow/135719702.cms" rel="noopener">Times of India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
