     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-11-10 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>India's Homegrown Bullet Train B28 Reaches 280 kmph in Testing</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Indian Railways</span> <span>#Bullet Train</span> <span>#Make In India</span> <span>#BEML</span></div>
       <p>India is advancing its high-speed rail ambitions with B28, the nation's first homegrown bullet train, currently taking shape at BEML’s Aditya Plant in Bengaluru. Designed for the Mumbai-Ahmedabad corridor, this eight-coach train measures 202 metres and is capable of carrying 557 passengers. During testing, B28 has already achieved speeds of 280 kmph, exceeding its operational speed of 249 kmph, according to The Better India.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/indias-homegrown-bullet-train-b28-reaches-280-kmph-in-testin.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The B28 promises a passenger experience focused on comfort and modern amenities. Inside, travellers can expect ergonomic seats, convenient charging points, expansive wide windows, and efficient LED lighting. The train also features dedicated executive-class seating, alongside Wi-Fi-based entertainment and multilingual announcements to enhance the journey. Aerodynamic design elements are incorporated to reduce air resistance and mitigate tunnel pressure waves, while regenerative braking recovers energy as the train slows.</p><p>Inclusivity is a priority for the B28 design. The train includes wheelchair-friendly doors, accessible toilets, and Braille signage to assist passengers. Dedicated spaces are also provided for passengers with reduced mobility, ensuring a comfortable and accessible travel experience for all. Emergency talk-back units are installed for safety.</p><p>The future of India's high-speed rail network looks set for significant expansion. Two B28 sets can be coupled to form a 16-coach train, increasing capacity. Furthermore, the Budget 2026–27 has announced seven additional high-speed rail corridors, indicating a broader national commitment to this mode of transport.</p><p>Why it matters: This marks a significant step in India's indigenous high-speed rail technology and inclusive infrastructure development.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is thorough, covers all technical and amenity-based details, and maintains a professional tone.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://thebetterindia.com/videos/india-hits-280-kmph-with-bullet-train-12799218" rel="noopener">The Better India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
