     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-11-10 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>J&amp;K court acquits two youth after six years in UAPA case</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A Jammu and Kashmir special court has ordered the immediate release of Huzaif Firdous Bhat and Ajaz Ahmad Khan, who spent six years in detention under the UAPA and Arms Act. Presiding Judge Feroz Ahmad Khan ruled that suspicion cannot substitute for legal proof, noting that the prosecution failed to establish the essential ingredients of the charged offences beyond a reasonable doubt.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/jk-court-acquits-two-youth-after-six-years-in-uapa-case.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The pair was accused of transporting grenades and ammunition for terrorist activities in 2020. However, the court found the evidence unreliable and insufficient to sustain a conviction. Cleric Mirwaiz Umar Farooq criticized the prolonged detention, highlighting the human cost of indiscriminate arrests and the lack of accountability.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief is accurate and captures the legal rationale behind the acquittal and the subsequent reaction.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/jammu-and-kashmir/jk-court-releases-two-youth-after-6-years-says-suspicion-however-grave-cannot-substitute-proof/article71685216.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
