     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Mongabay India · 2026-11-10 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Kashmir Musk Deer Face Significant Habitat Loss Amid Climate Change</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Kashmir Musk Deer</span> <span>#Endangered Species</span> <span>#Climate Change</span> <span>#Himalaya</span></div>
       <p>The Kashmir musk deer, a small, solitary, and antlerless mountain species, is indigenous to the western Himalaya, with its range extending across parts of Afghanistan, India, and Pakistan. Notably, male Kashmir musk deer lack antlers but are equipped with elongated upper canines, which they primarily use for territorial defense and competition for mates during the breeding season.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/kashmir-musk-deer-face-significant-habitat-loss-amid-climate.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This species is currently classified as 'endangered' on the IUCN Red List. Its population faces severe threats from poaching, driven by demand for its musk gland, as well as habitat loss, fragmentation, and human disturbance. A new study conducted in the Kashmir Himalaya indicates that climate change is projected to further diminish suitable habitats and weaken connectivity between the remaining patches, thereby escalating the risk of population isolation.</p><p>Mohsin Javid, from the Department of Wildlife Sciences at Aligarh Muslim University (AMU) and the corresponding author of the study, emphasized that the Kashmir musk deer's distribution is not solely governed by climate. He stated, &quot;It is a forest-associated mountain species whose distribution is also shaped by elevation, ruggedness, forest structure and human disturbance.&quot; The study addressed a critical gap by assessing both habitat suitability and connectivity for the species, exploring whether future suitable areas would remain sufficiently connected to support dispersal, gene flow, recolonisation, and climate-driven movement.</p><p>Javid also highlighted the ecological significance of Kashmir musk deer, noting their role as selective herbivores and prey within Himalayan ecosystems. They influence plant communities and contribute to nutrient cycling, while also serving as potential prey for predators such as leopards. Consequently, conserving this species necessitates protecting not only the deer itself but also its broader ecosystem.</p><p>What to watch: Implementation of conservation strategies to maintain habitat connectivity.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article provides a comprehensive and accurate report based on the source text, correctly attributing quotes and context.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://india.mongabay.com/2026/11/kashmir-musk-deer-face-steep-habitat-losses-under-high-emissions-scenarios/" rel="noopener">Mongabay India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
