     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-11-10 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Kerala Proposes Allowing Karnataka-Registered Doctors to Practice in Kasaragod</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The Kerala government has recommended that doctors registered with the Karnataka Medical Council be permitted to practice in Kasaragod to address local staff shortages. Health Minister K. Muraleedharan stated the State Medical Council is expected to approve the measure soon. The government has also extended National Health Mission contracts until March 31 and plans to construct a new District Medical Office building for ₹4.8 crore.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/kerala-proposes-allowing-karnataka-registered-doctors-to-pra.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Additional infrastructure projects include establishing hostel facilities at the Kasaragod Medical College and a dedicated nursing student hostel. The government also proposed Periye as a site for an All India Institute of Medical Sciences and committed to ensuring forensic surgeon services at Kanhangad district hospital.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief effectively summarizes the government's proposal and planned infrastructure projects within the word limit.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/kerala/karnataka-registered-doctors-may-be-allowed-to-practise-in-kasaragod-keralam-health-minister/article71686180.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
