     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · News18 World · 2026-11-10 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Netanyahu Dials Trump 'Every Day', Pushes Him For Renewed US Strikes On Iran</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Benjamin Netanyahu</span> <span>#Donald Trump</span> <span>#Iran</span> <span>#Middle East</span></div>
       <p>Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu has been calling US President Donald Trump daily to push for a resumption of military action against Iran, according to a Financial Times report cited by News18 World. This lobbying effort occurs even as Arab and Muslim leaders intensify their efforts to persuade Washington to prioritize diplomacy.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/netanyahu-dials-trump-every-day-pushes-him-for-renewed-us-st.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The Financial Times report, citing a person briefed on the discussions, indicated that regional leaders are urging Trump not to escalate the conflict and instead prioritize negotiations with Tehran. This push comes amid growing concerns that a renewed US offensive could plunge the region into a deeper crisis. A source quoted, “Netanyahu calls him every day [to urge him to resume the war]. Those who don’t want a war within the White House and outside have been trying to combat that.” The source added, “The regional leaders are telling him not to escalate and prioritise negotiations.”</p><p>Netanyahu, serving as caretaker Prime Minister after Israel’s inconclusive elections, has been lobbying for renewed US-Israeli strikes aimed at forcing the collapse of Iran’s regime. However, a person familiar with the situation in Israel noted that while Netanyahu desired military action to resume, there was currently no “push” to persuade Trump to launch another offensive.</p><p>Trump’s remarks following a meeting with Qatari Prime Minister Sheikh Mohammed bin Abdulrahman al-Thani at the White House suggested a possible shift towards diplomacy. The US President stated on his Truth Social platform that there was “positive momentum” towards ending the conflict and implied another round of American strikes against Iran was “quite unlikely.” He maintained that the US was “locked and loaded and ready to go,” but added that further military action “seems to be quite unlikely.”</p><p>Trump also noted that Qatar, Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates (UAE), Bahrain, Kuwait, and other countries preferred maintaining the status quo, referring to the US naval blockade and Operation Iron Gate. A diplomat briefed on the discussions described the meeting between Trump, Vice-President JD Vance, and Sheikh Mohammed as “positive,” confirming an “agreement that diplomacy is the preferred way forward to get a deal.”</p><p>At least four Arab and Muslim leaders, including Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman, UAE President Sheikh Mohamed bin Zayed al-Nahyan, Qatar’s Emir Sheikh Tamim bin Hamad al-Thani, and Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdoğan, have spoken to Trump in recent days, urging him to hold off on fresh strikes. Concerns were particularly heightened that Trump might launch a new offensive after his Republican Party’s poor performance in last Tuesday’s midterm elections, where the unpopularity of the US-Israeli war against Iran and its impact on fuel prices were key election issues.</p><p>There is a broad consensus among Arab and Muslim leaders that renewed military escalation could trigger severe Iranian retaliation targeting their countries. Anwar Gargash, diplomatic adviser to the UAE President, emphasized that the priority must remain “the pursuit of political diplomatic solutions capable of producing a more sustainable regional balance.” He expressed hope that the US midterm election outcome would reinforce diplomatic efforts for lasting peace and stability in the Gulf. Iran, meanwhile, has warned it is prepared to respond to any fresh attacks, threatening to target undersea cables in the Strait of Hormuz and data centers in the region, besides expanding the conflict beyond the Middle East.</p><p>What to watch: The ongoing diplomatic efforts versus calls for military action in the Middle East.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article effectively synthesizes the report on Netanyahu's lobbying and the broader regional diplomatic efforts, accurately reflecting the source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.news18.com/world/netanyahu-calls-donald-trump-daily-to-push-for-iran-strikes-us-israel-iran-war-ws-l-10420679.html" rel="noopener">News18 World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
