  <nav class="catrow"><span class="catgrp"><b>News</b><a href="/category/national">national</a><a href="/category/world">world</a><a href="/category/defence">defence</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Money</b><a href="/category/business">business</a><a href="/category/finance">finance</a><a href="/category/education">education</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Sports</b><a href="/category/sports">sports</a><a href="/category/cricket">cricket</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Culture</b><a href="/category/entertainment">entertainment</a><a href="/category/positive">positive</a><a href="/category/funny">funny</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Life</b><a href="/category/science">science</a><a href="/category/environment">environment</a><a href="/category/space">space</a><a href="/category/health">health</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Tech</b><a href="/category/tech">tech</a><a href="/category/ai">ai</a></span></nav></div><div class="gtr"><span>Read in your language:</span><div id="google_translate_element"></div></div><div class="wrap"><main id="content"><p class="crumb"><a href="/">← Front page</a> · <a href="/archive">Archive</a> · <a href="/category/finance">finance</a></p>
     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-11-10 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>NPCI's UPI MDR: What it Means for Savers and Merchants</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Personal Finance</span> <span>#UPI</span> <span>#MDR</span> <span>#NPCI</span></div>
       <p>The National Payments Corporation of India (NPCI) is set to implement Merchant Discount Rate (MDR) charges on select Person-to-Merchant (P2M) UPI transactions exceeding ₹2,000. While the implementation was initially deferred beyond its original 15 October start date to avoid additional costs during the festive season, NPCI stated on 13 October that the revised charges would be implemented immediately post-Diwali, with a separate effective date to be announced. Merchants and customers are currently awaiting clarity, with a rollout likely in November.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/npcis-upi-mdr-what-it-means-for-savers-and-merchants.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Under the proposed structure, an MDR of 0.4% will apply to eligible P2M UPI transactions over ₹2,000. For larger transactions, a cap of ₹300 will be applied for payments of ₹75,000 and above. For example, a ₹1 lakh payment would incur a ₹300 charge instead of ₹400. Importantly, this MDR is a merchant-side transaction fee, meaning the cost will be borne by the merchant receiving the payment, not the customer making the UPI transaction.</p><p>Certain merchant categories will benefit from concessional rates. Railways, telecom services, insurance, and fuel will have a flat MDR of ₹5 per transaction for payments exceeding ₹2,000. Capital market transactions, including mutual funds and securities, will see a concessional MDR of 0.02%, capped at ₹300. Crucially, person-to-person (P2P) UPI transfers, such as sending money to family or splitting bills, will continue to remain free of charge.</p><p>Savers and taxpayers should note that this change primarily impacts businesses accepting larger UPI payments, not individual users for personal transfers. The exact implementation date and other related details are still awaited from NPCI, though the indication is for a post-Diwali rollout, potentially this month.</p><p>What to watch: Official announcement from NPCI regarding the exact MDR implementation date.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is comprehensive, accurate, and utilizes all provided figures and context from the source text effectively.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.livemint.com/money/personal-finance/upi-charges-after-diwali-will-customers-pay-extra-on-payments-above-2-000-npcis-mdr-rollout-likely-in-nov-11794289795387.html" rel="noopener">Livemint Money</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
