     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-10 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Osaka University-Led Team Designs Flexible Molecules for Efficient Orange-to-Red OLED Light</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#OLEDs</span> <span>#Materials Science</span> <span>#Light Emission</span> <span>#Osaka University</span></div>
       <p>Researchers led by the University of Osaka have designed flexible, metal-free molecules that efficiently generate orange-to-red light for organic light-emitting diode (OLED) displays. This development addresses the long-standing challenge of creating metal-free light-emitting materials that produce these specific colors efficiently, as most high-performance OLEDs currently rely on expensive metals like iridium or platinum, according to Phys.org. The team demonstrated that materials do not require a rigid shape to function effectively, with flexible molecules capable of changing shape also emitting orange-to-red light.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/osaka-university-led-team-designs-flexible-molecules-for-eff.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The new materials operate on the principle of thermally activated delayed fluorescence (TADF), specifically via through-space charge transfer (TSCT). Traditionally, such molecules are designed to be rigid to maintain the close proximity of their electron-donating and electron-accepting parts. However, the Osaka-led team, including collaborators from Europe, designed large, complex molecules with movable segments. They discovered these molecules efficiently emit orange-to-red light through this flexible mechanism, as detailed in a working paper posted to the ChemRxiv preprint server. Lead author Nae Aota stated that time-resolved spectroscopic techniques and quantum chemical calculations revealed light emission is governed by both pre-absorption shapes and post-excitation changes. The new molecules have an &quot;S&quot; shape with rotatable connections, allowing various conformations favorable for TSCT, even without permanent conformational locking.</p><p>Senior author Youhei Takeda reported that OLEDs crafted with these molecules produced orange-to-red light with an external quantum efficiency of up to 15%. Takeda noted that the best device maintained efficiency even at high brightness, signifying a &quot;relatively high level of performance for such materials.&quot; This achievement, Takeda added, was facilitated through international collaborations with Professor Przemyslaw Data under the Horizon Europe program and with Professors Marc Etherington and Piotr de Silva through JST's ASPIRE program, combining complementary expertise to gain deeper insights into molecular flexibility and light emission.</p><p>This innovative molecular design, leveraging flexibility instead of rigidification, offers new understanding into the connection between molecular structure, rearrangements, and light emission. The team anticipates further development will lead to highly efficient TADF materials emitting at even longer wavelengths, with potential applications extending beyond OLED displays to include biological imaging. The original article, reviewed by Lisa Lock and Andrew Zinin, was posted on November 10, 2026.</p><p>What to watch: Future development for longer wavelength TADF materials.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive coverage of the source material with accurate technical details and quotes.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-team-molecules-efficiently-generate-orange.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
