     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · TechCrunch · 2026-11-10 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge conf" title="Reported by TechCrunch, Engadget">confirmed by 2 sources</span></div>
       <h1>Reddit is turning user conversations into AI-powered product reviews</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Reddit</span> <span>#AI</span> <span>#Product Reviews</span> <span>#Technology</span></div>
       <p>Reddit is debuting &quot;Reddit Reviews,&quot; a new feature that organizes user conversations about products into dedicated, AI-powered reviews, aiming to make the platform a key destination for product research, as reported by TechCrunch. This initiative builds on how users already leverage Reddit to learn about products from firsthand accounts and recommendations. Engadget describes this as the platform's new toolset collecting and organizing user data on thousands of items.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/reddit-is-turning-user-conversations-into-ai-powered-product.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The feature taps into AI to summarize highlights and key takeaways from user experiences across Reddit communities. These summaries detail what users like and dislike, how products perform, potential failures, or trade-offs to consider before buying. This helps consumers determine if a particular product is suitable for their needs. The company has been testing this product reviews feature since February, initially with U.S. users in categories such as Electronics, Video Games, and Health &amp; Beauty products. In September, the test expanded to include Home &amp; Garden and Fashion &amp; Apparel categories.</p><p>Reddit's announcement acknowledges the potential for &quot;astroturfing&quot;—faking public support—given the rise of generative AI and bots. To counter this, Reddit plans to utilize its existing rules, recent measures against automated abuse and spam, and community moderators to ensure the legitimacy of reviews. The company states that Reddit Reviews is now rolling out to all U.S. users on iOS, Android, and web, starting with an initial selection of around 10,000 products. Reddit intends to expand the feature to more categories and beyond the U.S. over time.</p><p>To use the feature, Reddit users can search for a product or category within the app’s search bar or click on a highlighted product name in a comment. Users are then directed to a screen that summarizes important conversations under a “what people are saying” heading. This includes a visual breakdown indicating the sentiment—positive, negative, or mixed—and specific points users liked or disliked about the item. For example, a user researching an espresso machine might find information on counter space requirements or ease of use for beginners. Users can then browse comments, filter them by topic, or access the original conversation to ask further questions or seek advice. The feature also allows users to navigate from a single product review back to the full category to compare similar products.</p><p>The timing of this feature launch is strategic, arriving just ahead of the busy holiday shopping season, a period when consumers typically dedicate significant time to online product research for major purchases.</p><p>What to watch: The expansion of Reddit Reviews to more products and international markets.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft provides a clear and accurate summary of the new feature, its purpose, and the company's approach to mitigating potential issues like astroturfing.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>How this was reported · 2 sources</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/10/reddit-is-turning-user-conversations-into-ai-powered-product-reviews/" rel="noopener">TechCrunch</a></li><li><a href="https://www.engadget.com/2310918/reddit-is-sort-of-getting-into-the-product-reviews-business/" rel="noopener">Engadget</a></li></ul>
      <p>Synthesised from 2 independent reports on the same event. Details are attributed to the outlet that carried them; nothing outside these sources is published.</p>
    </div>
       
