     <article class="story single" data-beat="entertainment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENTERTAINMENT</span> · TOI Entertainment · 2026-11-10 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Saba reveals why mom Sharmila converted to Islam when she married Tiger Pataudi</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Sharmila Tagore</span> <span>#Saba Ali Khan Pataudi</span> <span>#Mansoor Ali Khan Pataudi</span> <span>#Islam</span></div>
       <p>Veteran actor Sharmila Tagore converted to Islam and adopted the name Ayesha to marry legendary cricketer Mansoor Ali Khan Pataudi, known as Tiger Pataudi. Their marriage garnered significant attention, with Tagore's conversion becoming a subject of controversy. The couple reportedly received death threats following their wedding, according to TOI Entertainment.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/saba-reveals-why-mom-sharmila-converted-to-islam-when-she-ma.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Years later, their younger daughter, jewellery designer and tarot reader Saba Ali Khan Pataudi, discussed her mother's decision. During a conversation with Siddharth Kannan, Saba explained that the conversion was a &quot;necessity&quot; for the marriage to be formally recognized under Mansoor's family's customs and traditions. She suggested her paternal grandmother would not have approved otherwise. Saba stated, “In those days, conversion was necessary. The children are legitimate because of conversion, which legalised things. It was a necessity and not something one could consider if or not to do.” She clarified that certain religious customs applied when a Muslim man married someone from another faith, and without conversion, the marriage would not have been accepted within the family. “My grandmother (Mansoor’s mother) would surely not have allowed it in any other way,” Saba added.</p><p>Saba also clarified that her mother's conversion did not hinder her acting career or professional identity. She noted that Mansoor was “liberated in his thoughts” and did not impose restrictions on Sharmila after their marriage. Neither Mansoor nor his family insisted that the actor change her name on official documents, which could have impacted her professional life. Saba reiterated, “It didn’t take away from her personality or her profession.”</p><p>Regarding the family's religious beliefs, Saba revealed that religion was never imposed on the children. While she personally follows Islam, like her father, the family celebrated festivals from various faiths, including Holi, Diwali, and Eid. Saba emphasized that both her parents allowed their children the freedom to follow individual beliefs and practices, stating, “We were always allowed to follow our own path by both our parents.”</p><p>What to watch: The family's approach to interfaith relationships and personal beliefs.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately captures the details of the source, including the specific reasoning behind the conversion and the family's approach to religion.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/entertainment/hindi/bollywood/news/sharmila-tagore-converted-to-islam-when-she-married-mansoor-ali-khan-pataudi-because-it-was-necessary-in-those-days-reveals-saba-pataudi-my-grandmother-wouldnt-have-allowed-it-any-other-way/articleshow/135712579.cms" rel="noopener">TOI Entertainment</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
