       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-10 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Simulations offer explanation for Webb’s mysterious 'little red dots'</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Astronomers have utilized advanced simulations to identify the nature of 'little red dots' (LRDs) captured by the James Webb Space Telescope. The findings, published in The Astrophysical Journal Letters, suggest these compact, bright objects are primeval galaxies containing massive black holes born from collapsing stars.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/simulations-offer-explanation-for-webbs-mysterious-little-re.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>These black holes, estimated at 10 million solar masses, remain shrouded in dense gas disks that obscure X-ray emissions while allowing specific light signatures to escape. The model successfully explains the objects' unusual spectral properties and dense star clusters, solving a major mystery regarding the early universe.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief effectively summarizes the complex scientific explanation for LRDs while remaining within the word count.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-webb-controversial-red-dots-explanation.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
