     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Economic Times · 2026-11-10 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Tata Electronics Ordered to Review Safety After Three Deaths at Krishnagiri Plant</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Business</span> <span>#Tata Electronics</span> <span>#Safety</span> <span>#Accident</span></div>
       <p>Apple supplier Tata Electronics has been instructed to conduct a comprehensive review of the performance and safety compliance of agencies maintaining its Effluent Treatment Plant (ETP) in Krishnagiri, following the deaths of three contract workers. Krishnagiri district collector C Dinesh Kumar confirmed to ET that the company must submit a detailed Action Taken Report (ATR) outlining the accident's circumstances, identified lapses, corrective measures, and a preventive action plan. The district administration is closely monitoring the situation, ensuring necessary safety measures are implemented before the ETP resumes operations. Apple is also reportedly probing the deaths.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/tata-electronics-ordered-to-review-safety-after-three-deaths.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The incident on Monday evening saw three contract workers allegedly die from asphyxiation at the plant. Officials from the Tamil Nadu Pollution Control Board (TNPCB) and the Directorate of Industrial Safety and Health (DISH) are conducting a detailed safety inspection, focusing on procedures for confined-space entry, gas monitoring, protective equipment, and emergency rescue arrangements. While there is no current proposal to shut down the entire Tata Electronics plant, the ETP's operations will remain suspended until authorities verify compliance with required safety measures and provide clearance.</p><p>Collector Kumar explained the chain of events: NALCO Water International, entrusted with ETP maintenance, engaged four workers through Permacorp. On Monday, while cleaning the exterior of a nanofiltration tank, one worker reportedly looked inside, inhaled toxic gases, lost consciousness, and fell in. The supervisor, Kaveri, directed an electrician to rescue him, but the electrician also lost consciousness and fell. The supervisor then entered and suffered a similar fate. A fourth worker sought assistance, but tragically, three workers died in the incident.</p><p>Tamil Nadu industries minister S Keerthana expressed condolences to the families, directing the district administration to provide support and address the situation with urgency. She emphasized the paramount importance of worker safety and dignity, stating that the tragic incident would be thoroughly investigated, and appropriate action taken based on findings to prevent recurrence.</p><p>What to watch: The findings of the safety inspections and the implementation of corrective actions by Tata Electronics.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft covers all key aspects, including the specific sequence of events, the entities involved, and the regulatory response, accurately reflecting the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/cons-products/electronics/tata-electronics-directed-to-submit-detailed-action-taken-report-after-deaths-of-three-workers-at-treatment-plant/articleshow/135701001.cms" rel="noopener">Economic Times</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
