     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · The Hindu World · 2026-11-10 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>U.S.-Brokered Peace Talks for Ukraine, Russia Begin in Abu Dhabi</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Ukraine</span> <span>#Russia</span> <span>#United States</span> <span>#Peace Talks</span></div>
       <p>Three-way talks between Russia, Ukraine, and the United States commenced on Tuesday, November 10, 2026, in Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates. This two-day session marks the first such meeting since February and aims to renew peace efforts following earlier negotiations this year. The discussions are primarily focused on security guarantees.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/us-brokered-peace-talks-for-ukraine-russia-begin-in-abu-dhab.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Jared Kushner, son-in-law to U.S. President Donald Trump, stated on X that he and fellow envoy Steve Witkoff are working towards a &quot;negotiated solution that can deliver lasting peace and create the conditions for a sovereign, secure, and prosperous Ukraine within a more stable European security environment.” Ahead of these talks, Indian Prime Minister Modi spoke with Ukrainian President Zelenskyy, while External Affairs Minister Jaishankar engaged with Russian Foreign Minister Lavrov. India has also expressed readiness to mediate a grain safety agreement for &quot;food corridors.&quot;</p><p>Despite the renewed diplomatic push, Moscow and Kyiv maintain divergent demands as the full-scale war approaches its five-year mark. Russian President Vladimir Putin insists Ukraine withdraw troops from the four regions Moscow illegally annexed in September 2022—Donetsk, Luhansk, Zaporizhzhia, and Kherson—and abandon its NATO bid, accept military limits, and lift restrictions on the Russian language and the Moscow-affiliated Orthodox Church. He rejects a general halt in hostilities, arguing it would allow Ukrainian forces to regroup. Conversely, Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy rejects ceding territory, imposing limits on Ukraine's military, or restricting its arrangements with Western partners, which Kyiv views as essential security guarantees against future invasions. Zelenskyy advocates for an immediate ceasefire as a prerequisite for wider settlement talks, believing a direct meeting with Putin is the only path to resolving territorial issues.</p><p>Both sides have dispatched experienced negotiators. Russia's delegation is led by Putin aide Vladimir Medinsky, who has headed Moscow's team since the March 2022 Istanbul talks, and includes Deputy Foreign Ministers Sergei Ryabkov and Mikhail Galuzin, military intelligence chief Igor Kostyukov, and deputy chief of the General Staff's operational department Yuri Zhigarlovsky. Ukraine's team is led by Rustem Umerov, head of its Foreign Intelligence Service, with reports also including Zelenskyy's chief of staff Kyrylo Budanov, his deputy Sergiy Kyslytsya, and Davyd Arakhamia, parliamentary faction leader of the Servant of the People party. Meanwhile, Russia and Ukraine have escalated aerial attacks.</p><p>What to watch: The progress on security guarantees and potential for direct high-level engagement.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article is well-structured, covers all provided factual details, and accurately reflects the provided source material.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/us-brokered-talks-are-set-to-start-in-abu-dhabi-to-discuss-ukraine-russia-peace-deal/article71685183.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
