     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · TechCrunch · 2026-11-10 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>X-energy Receives NRC Permit for Four Texas Nuclear Reactors</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#X-energy</span> <span>#Nuclear</span> <span>#SMR</span> <span>#NRC</span></div>
       <p>X-energy announced Monday it has secured a construction permit from the U.S. Nuclear Regulatory Commission (NRC) to build its first four commercial small modular nuclear reactors (SMRs). The approval allows X-energy to construct its Xe-100 reactors at a Dow chemical plant located in Texas. This marks a significant step for the nuclear startup, with each reactor designed to generate 80 megawatts of electricity and 200 megawatts of heat. The U.S. nuclear industry has increasingly pinned its hopes on SMRs, like those X-energy is developing, with proponents suggesting they will speed up construction and reduce costs, though these advantages remain unproven.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-10/x-energy-receives-nrc-permit-for-four-texas-nuclear-reactors.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The NRC's decision represents the first approval for an advanced nuclear reactor at an industrial site within the United States. X-energy states its Xe-100 reactor design, which utilizes helium gas flowing over uranium-containing fuel pellets, is inherently safer than many existing reactors. However, the Union of Concerned Scientists (UCS) has publicly questioned both this safety claim and the expediency with which X-energy received the permit. Edwin Lyman, director of nuclear power safety for the organization, issued a statement asserting, &quot;The NRC only accomplished this feat by pushing consideration of critical safety questions to a later date—issues the NRC should’ve fully resolved before green lighting construction.&quot; The company anticipates these initial reactors at the Dow plant will not become operational until the early 2030s, underscoring that no SMRs are currently in operation anywhere in the U.S.</p><p>The nuclear industry has recently benefited from robust interest, particularly from hyperscalers and data center developers, who are actively seeking diverse and stable sources of electricity. This enthusiasm is reflected in X-energy's financing, where Amazon’s Climate Pledge Fund played a leading role in its $500 million Series C-1 funding round. Beyond this investment, the tech giant Amazon has also committed to buying 5 gigawatts of capacity from the nuclear startup, signaling substantial long-term support. Riding this wave of excitement, X-energy successfully raised $1 billion during its initial public offering in April of this year.</p><p>Despite the recent regulatory milestone, investor optimism surrounding X-energy has tempered since its IPO. The company’s shares are presently trading at less than half their initial offering price, although the new permit approval did provide a small jolt, helping the stock recover some of its losses from the previous week. The inherent challenges of nuclear power plant development, including high construction costs and lengthy completion timelines, contribute to investor hesitancy. While X-energy ultimately aims to mitigate both the time and cost factors, its first reactors are widely expected to be expensive projects.</p><p>What to watch: Future construction milestones and ongoing regulatory oversight of X-energy’s advanced nuclear projects.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately synthesizes the provided source and full text, covering the permit approval, safety controversy, financial details, and market context without unnecessary padding.</p>
